# {{STORY_TITLE}}

## Story

As {{ROLE}} I want {{CAPABILITY}} so that {{BENEFIT}}.

## Acceptance criteria

One scenario per criterion: `#### <key>`, then Given / When / Then steps with concrete values.
The key is lowercase and hyphenated and names the behaviour — never a number, because a number
means nothing once the list is reordered. Group scenarios under `### Rule:` headings where the
story has business rules; every rule gets at least one scenario. One `When` per scenario. Every
key needs one end-user test before the build stage may run. The shorter line form
`- <key>: <criterion>` is read as well.

### Rule: {{BUSINESS_RULE}}

#### {{CRITERION_KEY}}
- Given {{STARTING_STATE}}
- When {{ONE_TRIGGER}}
- Then {{OBSERVABLE_OUTCOME}}

## Out of scope

What this story deliberately does not deliver, with the story or reason that owns it. Leave the
section out when there is nothing to say.

- {{NOT_IN_THIS_STORY}}

## Changed expectations

Only when the story changes behaviour the system already has: one line per expectation that no
longer holds, in the project's language — what the user or caller sees now, and what they see
after. No story ids and no file names; the plan stage finds the tests. Leave the section out when
the story only adds behaviour.

- {{WHAT_CHANGES}}

## Assumptions

Everything the story rests on that only the domain contact can confirm. Each line is a
question, answered or open — this is the asynchronous channel to the domain expert, not a
place for decisions the team took itself.

- open: {{QUESTION}}
